### Checklist

1. **Headshot.** Use a recent photo where you are looking at the camera and not scowling. Plain or light background. No hard hat unless it is a deliberate choice for the sector signal. The face should be the main thing in the frame.
2. **Custom URL.** Set to linkedin.com/in/zander-demo or as close as available. Go to Edit public profile and URL in the top right of your profile. A clean URL looks professional in outreach and on the Profit Coach page.
3. **Company page.** Link the Profit Coach company page to your current role so it shows the logo and is searchable. If a Harlow & Pike page exists, link that to your Operations Director role too. Gaps in company links look like something is being hidden.
4. **Contact info.** Open your contact settings to 2nd degree connections at minimum. Add the practice email once it is set up, and the Profit Coach page URL as your website. An MD who wants to reach out after reading the About section should not have to hunt for a way in.
5. **Job titles.** Use LinkedIn's job title dropdown for each role so the titles index correctly in search. Operations Director, Contracts Manager, and Project Engineer are all searchable terms. Current role should read something like Business Coach, Building Services and M&E Contractors so it appears in relevant searches.

===== conversations:followup (69s, 9628 chars)
Most replies from M&E and HVAC MDs are not yes or no. They are tests. Handle each one with a question, not a pitch.

### When they reply

**They say: Yes, interested**

```text
Good to hear, {first_name}. The quickest way to make it useful is a twenty-minute call where I ask you a few questions about how the business runs at the moment. No pitch, just a honest look at where the margin or the time is going. I have slots on Mondays and Thursdays from 1pm. Does either day work for you this week or next?
```

_Move straight to a call. Do not send a document or a link at this stage._

**They say: What is it exactly?**

```text
Fair question. I work with MDs in M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting, usually somewhere between £2m and £12m turnover. The work focuses on three things: getting proper visibility on job margin before the final account closes, getting retentions chased and cash moving, and getting the MD out of the middle of every decision. I do that by getting into the numbers and the operation from week one, not after a learning period. I only carry eight clients at a time so it stays hands-on. Easiest way to know if it fits your situation is a twenty-minute call. I have time on Monday or Thursday afternoon if either works.
```

_Keep it short. Answer the question with specifics, then move to a call._

**They say: How much is it?**

```text
Aye, straight to it, I respect that. The full programme runs up to £50,000 a year for some clients, but you can start for under £2,000 a month, which is where most building services contractors begin. To give you an honest answer on what makes sense for your business, I need to understand what you're dealing with first. One recovered retention or one job that doesn't bleed margin typically covers the fee. Worth a twenty-minute call to find out if the numbers stack up for you?
```

_Use the anchor before naming the minimum. Do not apologise for the fee._

**They say: Not right now**

```text
No problem at all, {first_name}. Timing matters. Can I ask, is it a case of genuinely busy and not the right moment, or is there something specific that needs to shift first before it would make sense?
```

_Neutral is not no. One question buys you the real reason. Do not send a brochure or a follow-up pack._

**They say: We're doing fine**

```text
Glad to hear it. Most of the MDs I talk to would say the same, and a lot of them are right. The ones where it's worth a conversation tend to have turnover going up but take-home staying flat, or they can't quite say which jobs are making money and which aren't. If neither of those is a live issue, you probably don't need me. If either one rings a bell, worth ten minutes to find out.
```

_Do not challenge their view. Plant the doubt with specifics and leave the door open._

**They say: Who are you?**

```text
Fair enough. I spent fifteen years in building services, finishing as Operations Director at Harlow & Pike where I grew the HVAC division from £4.2m to £11.8m. I did that by tightening job pricing and shifting the model from one-off installs to planned maintenance contracts. I now work with MDs in M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting who are winning decent work but can't work out where the margin's going, and who can't step away because everything still runs through them. I work with eight contractors at a time so it stays proper rather than just another coaching programme. Does any of that match what you're dealing with?
```

_Answer directly. Proof first, then a question to find out if it lands._

**They say: something neutral like 'interesting' or 'ok'**

```text
Good to know it landed. Quick question: is job margin visibility something you're actively trying to fix at the moment, or more something that's been on the list for a while?
```

_One question. Find out where they are before offering anything._

**They say: Send me some info**

```text
Happy to. Before I send anything, it'll be more useful if I know a bit about where you are. Two quick questions: what's the main thing costing you margin or time at the moment, and roughly what size is the business in terms of turnover and team? That way I can send you something relevant rather than a generic pack. If you'd rather just talk it through, I have Monday and Thursday afternoons free.
```

_Do not fire off a PDF cold. Get two pieces of information first. If they push back and just want the link, send the BOSS Scorecard._

### Avoid

- Sending a brochure or PDF before you know what they need.
- Pitching the system before asking a single question about their business.
- Apologising for the fee or offering a discount to soften a price question.
- Treating a neutral reply as rejection and moving on.
- Asking more than one question at a time.
- Following up within hours of a reply. Give them room.
- Using coaching language: transformation, journey, outcomes, framework.
- Telling them what the problem probably is before they have described it.

### VIP nurture: top 100

> **Who goes on the list and how often to contact them:** The top 100 are MDs or owners of M&E, HVAC or electrical contracting firms who have connected but not yet replied, or who replied neutrally and went quiet. They are the right size (£2m to £12m), in the right sector, and in the right geography. Send something useful every two weeks. Never sell. Each message gives away one tool, names one specific pain, and ends with one small offer. Personalise the first line every time.

**VIP nurture 1: Profit Maximiser**

```text
{personalisation}

One thing I use with building services contractors that tends to land well is a table I call the Profit Maximiser. It shows what happens to profit when you cut expenses at different margin levels.

Here's why it matters on a contracting business:

1️⃣ At a 10% net margin, cutting expenses by 11% doubles your profit. Not your turnover. Your profit.
2️⃣ Most MDs focus on winning more work. The table shows that a tighter grip on job costs often moves the number faster.
3️⃣ It takes about five minutes to run through your own figures and see where the biggest gain sits.

I've put together a version with a worked example from a building services business so the numbers are in the right ballpark. Worth a look?

[link]
```

_Send to MDs who have connected but gone quiet. Follow up three days later with the five-step expense review question: 'Does this help me get or keep clients?'_

**VIP nurture 2: Cashflow Forecaster**

```text
{personalisation}

Cash flow tends to be the thing that catches M&E contractors sideways, especially when three retentions are sitting idle and a VAT bill lands at the same time.

I put together a simple forecaster that gives contractors a rolling view of where the cash is coming from and when. There's a phrase that gets used a lot in the industry: revenue is vanity, profit is sanity, cash is king. The forecaster makes the last one visible before it becomes a problem.

Three things it gives you:

1️⃣ A clear picture of what's due in, when, and from which jobs or retentions.
2️⃣ Early sight of the months where the position tightens, so you can act in advance rather than scramble.
3️⃣ A structure for whoever in the office should own the chasing, rather than it sitting with the MD.

Happy to send it across if it's useful.

[link]
```

_Send to MDs who have mentioned retentions or cash as a concern, or who went quiet after an early reply about cash being tight._

**VIP nurture 3: Time Value Tracker**

```text
{personalisation}

One of the first things I do with a new client is look at where the MD is actually spending their week. Not what they think they're spending it on. What they're actually spending it on.

There's a simple tool I use called the Time Value Tracker. It sorts the week into three types of work: £10 tasks, £100 tasks, and £1,000 tasks.

For most MDs in building services it shows the same picture:

1️⃣ A big chunk of the week sitting in £10 and £100 work, answering calls, approving orders, sorting things that someone else should own.
2️⃣ The £1,000 work, pricing strategy, key client relationships, job review, getting pushed to evenings or not done at all.
3️⃣ A straightforward way to see which tasks to shift down, and to whom, to get two or three hours a day back.

If that sounds familiar, it's worth ten minutes with the tracker. I can send it across.

[link]
```

_Send to MDs who have mentioned being stretched, unable to step away, or stuck in the day-to-day. Works well as the third nurture message after Profit Maximiser and Cashflow Forecaster._

### Nurture rhythm

- **Week 2:** Send Profit Maximiser. First VIP nurture message. Personalise the opening line. Give away the tool with no strings. End with a low-pressure offer to send the worked example.
- **Week 5:** Send Cashflow Forecaster. Second VIP nurture message. Reference retentions if they mentioned cash in any earlier exchange. Otherwise keep it general but specific to the sector.
- **Week 8:** Send Time Value Tracker. Third VIP nurture message. By this point you have given three useful things with no ask. If there is still no reply, add one line: 'Happy to have a twenty-minute call if any of this is relevant. Mondays or Thursdays from 1pm work for me.'
- **Day 90:** Check-in with one direct question. Send a short, plain message. No tool. No link. Something like: 'Hi {first_name}, checking in. Is margin or cash flow still something you're working on, or has it shifted to something else?' One question. No preamble. If they reply, move to a call. If not, move them to a lighter monthly touch and free the slot for a warmer prospect.

===== sales:value (75s, 11055 chars)
Zander's value session is called the Margin & Cash Clarity Call. In 30 minutes, Mike walks away knowing exactly which area of his business is bleeding margin or locking up cash, and with two or three specific things he can act on before the week is out.

- **Name:** The Margin & Cash Clarity Call
- **Length:** 30 minutes
- **Who it is for:** MDs and owners of M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting firms turning over £2m to £12m, winning decent work but not understanding where the margin goes
- **What they get:** A straight-talking look at their job margin, retention position and owner dependency, scored red, amber or green, with two or three things they can act on immediately
- **The promise:** Real clarity on where the money is going and what to fix first. No fluff, no pitch, no catch.

### The questions

| Area | Question | Red looks like | Green looks like |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Job margin visibility | When a job closes, how quickly can you tell me what you actually made on it, and how confident are you in that number? | Final accounts take weeks to reconcile, numbers are inconsistent, and the MD suspects the figure is wrong but can't prove it | Job margin is visible within a week of close, reconciled against the original estimate, and the team trusts the number |
| In-job early warning | On a live contract running right now, could you tell me today whether it is ahead or behind on margin, and by how much? | No live visibility. Problems only surface when the final account is done and it is too late to recover anything | Weekly job cost reviews flag overspends early, variations are raised and agreed before the margin is gone |
| Pricing discipline | When the order book looked thin last winter, how did you price the work you put out to keep the lads busy? | Priced tight to win, margin sacrificed to keep utilisation up, no analysis of whether those jobs covered cost | Minimum margin thresholds held regardless of order book pressure, with a clear view of what each scheme needs to stack up |
| Retention tracking | How much retention money do you have outstanding right now, and who is responsible for chasing each one? | No tracker, no named owner, some holdbacks have been sitting for over a year with no active chase | Every retention is logged, has a named chaser, a target release date, and is reviewed on a set schedule |
| Owner dependency | In a typical week, what kind of decisions come to you that you think probably should not need you? | Site calls, materials approvals, client queries and subbie issues all escalate to the MD daily, consuming two to three hours or more | Contracts manager and supervisors handle day-to-day calls independently, MD is pulled in only when it genuinely needs them |
| Variation management | When scope changes on a live contract, what is the process for getting a variation agreed and on the application? | Variations are absorbed or raised late, main contractors push back, and extras get lost before they hit the application | Variation process is clear, extras are priced and submitted quickly, and the contracts manager owns the chase |
| Cash flow rhythm | Is there a month in the last year where you felt the cash position was tighter than it should have been, given the turnover? | Cash pressure in specific months, usually when retentions are slow, VAT falls due, or a large application is delayed | Cash flow is forecasted four to six weeks out, retention releases are planned in advance, and surprises are rare |
| Team and succession risk | If your best contracts manager or site supervisor handed in his notice this week, what would actually happen? | Most of the site knowledge and client relationships live in one or two people's heads, and losing one would be a serious problem | Processes and knowledge are documented, more than one person can cover key roles, and the MD is not the only relationship the main contractor trusts |
| Owner's own position | What did you actually take home last year, and how does that feel against the hours you put in? | Take-home flat or down despite rising turnover, hours are long, and the owner cannot identify a clear path to changing either | Profit growth tracks revenue growth, the owner has a target for take-home, and hours are reducing as the team develops |

### Your opening questions

1. What is the biggest thing stopping the business from being where you want it right now? Whether that is margin, cash, the team, or something else, just tell me what is sitting heaviest.
2. If we were having this same conversation twelve months from now, what would need to have changed for you to feel like this year was actually worth it?
3. Why does sorting that matter to you personally, beyond just the numbers? Because for most MDs I talk to, there is usually something behind it.
4. What have you already tried to fix it, and how did that go? I want to understand where you have been before we talk about where to go next.

### The magic question

**The magic question**

```text
Based on what you've told me, would it be useful if I showed you how I help contractors fix exactly this and get to a clear answer faster than they would on their own?
```

_Wait for the yes. Do not move until you have it._

**Bridge into the offer**

```text
Right. So what you've told me is that [their challenge in their words - e.g. 'the margin is disappearing on jobs and you can't pin down where']. And what you want, twelve months from now, is [their outcome in their words - e.g. 'to actually see what each job is making while you can still do something about it']. And personally, [their driver in their words - e.g. 'you want to stop working these hours and have something to show for it'].

Here is how I typically work with an MD in your position.

First, we get into the numbers properly in the first few weeks. Not a high-level overview - I mean job costing, pricing, retention position, the things that tell you what is actually happening. I have done this in a building services business myself, so there is no learning curve on my side.

Second, we build a weekly rhythm that keeps you and the team looking at the right numbers at the right time. Problems get caught at week four, not month twelve. That is where the margin gets recovered.

Third, we work on getting you out of the middle. The right decisions going to the right people, so you are not the escalation point for every call and every sign-off. That is where the hours come back.

I work with eight contractors at a time. Every conversation is grounded in contracts, retentions and site reality. No generic frameworks.

Does that sound like the kind of support that would actually make a difference for you?
```

_Tie each of the three points directly to what the owner said in the session. The 80% above is the structure. The 20% is their words fed back to them._

### The launch kit

**Launch email**

```text
Subject: Free 30-minute call for M&E and HVAC contractors: where is the margin actually going?

Hi [first_name],

If you are running an M&E or HVAC contracting business and the turnover is up but the profit is not following, this call is for you.

I am running a small number of free 30-minute Margin & Cash Clarity Calls for MDs in building services. In that half hour, I will look at three things with you:

- Where margin is leaking on jobs, and whether you have early warning before it is too late to do anything
- How much retention money is sitting unpaid, and whether anyone is accountable for chasing it
- Whether you are still the person every site decision escalates to, and what that is costing you in hours

You walk away with a clear picture of which area is capping the business right now, and two or three specific things you can act on straight away.

No fluff. No pitch. No catch.

I spent fifteen years in building services, finishing as Operations Director at Harlow & Pike where I grew the HVAC division from £4.2m to £11.8m. I know what a final account that does not stack up looks like, and I know where the money usually went.

I only run five of these a month, so spots go quickly.

[Booking link]

Zander
